Hey @praveendvd,
after a week of vacation I was finally able to look into this. It was a bit trickier than I thought. The problem was that spying [method classes](https://jestjs.io/docs/es6-class-mocks#mocking-a-specific-method-of-a-class) in this case didn't work because it seems like `jest.fn()` is not recognised as function in the prototype of the class. So what I had to do is write a new mock only for this file, e.g.:

```js
jest.mock('@wdio/config', () => ({
    ConfigParser: class ConfigParserMock {
        addConfigFile () {}
        autoCompile () {}
        getCapabilities () {}
    }
}))
```

which would allow me to mock on these function methods we use for the utility function. In order to mock these functions ahead of calling and initiating the `ConfigParser` you had to use `jest.spy` as follows:

```js
// import mock from above (this import doesn't actually import the real class)
import { ConfigParser } from '@wdio/config'

const addConfigFileMock = jest.spyOn(ConfigParser.prototype, 'addConfigFile')
addConfigFileMock.mockImplementationOnce(() => {
    const error: any = new Error('ups')
    error.code = 'MODULE_NOT_FOUND'
    throw error
})
```

With this we can keep the ConfigParser out of the picture for the tests we are writing. I saw for instance that you had a test for a js config file as well as a ts config file. This means you indirectly test the `autoCompile` method which is unimportant for the code we have been adding in this PR and which gets tested in other tests. Instead we can just check whether the method was called and don't care about what it actually does. This is very important in writing unit tests because you only care about the code you actually test and keep other interface independant from it, e.g.:

```js
const autoCompileMock = jest.spyOn(ConfigParser.prototype, 'autoCompile')
// call getCapabilities
expect(autoCompileMock).toBeCalledTimes(1)
```

I hope this was not too dragging for you and helped to understand jest and unit testing a bit better. I will go ahead and close this PR and would appreciate if you could review https://github.com/webdriverio/webdriverio/pull/8456

